  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 104 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nUpper-level ridging over the western CONUS and surface high pressure \nover the eastern CONUS remains prominent. These features will \nencourage warmer temperatures today and into the weekend. Today, \ntemperatures are expected to climb into the low 80s across much of \nthe area. Even warmer conditions are expected on Tuesday, especially \nin southern portions of the forecast area and into central Missouri \nwhere some locations could see high temperatures in the mid/upper \n80s. These temperatures would be well above normal--as much as 10-15 \ndegF! The normal high temperature at Sedalia (KDMO) on October 7, \nfor example, is 71 F...tomorrow's forecast high temperature \ncurrently sits at 85 F, a 14 degF difference.\n\nA weak surface front will sink south Wednesday afternoon and \nevening. Areas generally north of the Missouri River can expect to \nsee temperatures in the mid 70s on Thursday, and although this \nslightly closer to normal, it is still around 5 degF warmer. As for \nareas along and south of the Missouri River, temperatures are \nbroadly forecast to top out in the upper 70s and lower 80s with \nisolated locations in our southern fringes reaching the mid 80s. \nPoPs Thursday night into Friday morning across northern Missouri \nwere further reduced in this forecast cycle, now sitting below 5%. \nWhile a brief, isolated shower can't be totally ruled out, the low-\nlevel jet axis continues to favor areas in Nebraska and Iowa for \nshower development. \n\nThe subtle effects of the aforementioned boundary won't hang around \nfor long as the ridge expands eastward, bringing a rapid return of \nsoutherly surface flow and warm air advection. This will result in \nbuilding heat over the weekend, with temperatures ranging from the \nlower 80s in our north to the upper 80s in our southern tier of \ncounties. A more unsettled pattern appears to lie in wait for us \nnext week, most clearly in the form of a deepening upper trough \nalong the West Coast. Another feature to watch will be the evolution \nof Invest 92L which now has a near 100% chance of developing into a \ntropical cyclone in the next 48 hours. While substantial uncertainty \nremains, remnants of Invest 92L may propagate into portions of \ncentral and eastern Missouri as early as Sunday. As things stand in \nthe current guidance suite, it does not appear that this would bring \nany meaningful opportunity of rain to our area. Rather, our next \nbest chance of rain is more likely associated with the upper trough \nand its associated surface low, possibly bringing a cold front \nthrough the area early next week.",
